## 1. `resolvePaymentResult` — Return Shape Roulette

**Evidence:** 3 object-literal return shapes, lowest key overlap 0%, `{ code, message, ok }` vs `{ permanent, url }`.

The inferred return type is a bare union of three unrelated shapes with no discriminant. `ok` is `boolean` on two members and *absent* on the third, so `if (result.ok)` doesn't even compile against the union — and the natural agent response to that error is to widen the annotation (`any`, or a hand-written interface with every field optional), which is precisely how this degrades. The input is the same crime upstream: a bag of six optionals where `error: true` doesn't imply `code` and `message` are present, so `code` propagates into the result as `string | undefined`.

```ts
export type PaymentInput =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string; permanent: boolean }
  | { kind: "settled"; value: string };

export type PaymentResult =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string; permanent: boolean }
  | { kind: "success"; value: string; cached: boolean };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  switch (input.kind) {
    case "error":    return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
    case "redirect": return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: input.permanent };
    case "settled":  return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
  }
}
```

**What an agent gets wrong today.** Asked to *"add a retryable-failure case"*, it appends `return { ok: false, retryable: true }` — a fourth shape, no compiler complaint, and every caller branching on `code` silently misses it. There is no exhaustiveness check anywhere; the `switch` above adds one for free. Separately, line 29 is checked before line 30, so an input with **both** `error` and `redirect` set silently takes the error path. That precedence is undocumented and untested — an agent reordering the two guards changes behaviour and nothing catches it. The discriminated input makes the state legally unrepresentable instead of relying on guard order.

## 2. `describeCheckout` — Option Bag Junk Drawer

**Evidence:** parameter `options`, 6 distinct property reads.

The detector fires on breadth, not on `any` — `CheckoutOptions` *is* named and fully typed. The crime is that it's a junk drawer welding four unrelated concerns together: commerce identity (`plan`, `region`, `currency`), authorization (`role`), lifecycle (`status`), and transport policy (`retry`). Five of the six fields are `string`, so the type system cannot tell `plan` from `role`.

```ts
export interface CheckoutIdentity { plan: string; region: string; currency: string }
export interface CheckoutAttempt  { role: string; status: string; retry: boolean }

/** Stable key format: plan:role:region:currency:status:retry.
 *  Changing the field set or order invalidates every previously-emitted key. */
export function describeCheckout(id: CheckoutIdentity, attempt: CheckoutAttempt): string {
  return [id.plan, attempt.role, id.region, id.currency, attempt.status, String(attempt.retry)]
    .join(":");
}
```

Byte-for-byte identical output; the grouping is the whole change.

**What an agent gets wrong today.** Two ways, both of which compile. *(a)* Asked to *"include the coupon in the checkout description,"* it adds `coupon: string` to the flat interface and appends it to the array — silently changing the format of every key this function has ever produced. If those keys are cache keys or analytics dimensions, that's a cache-wide miss with no error. *(b)* Any reordering of the array — alphabetizing it, say — produces a compiling, wrong result, because six same-typed strings are mutually substitutable to the compiler. Grouping into two records makes an accidental swap across groups a type error, and the doc comment makes the output format a stated contract rather than an emergent one. If you want the within-group swaps caught too, brand the fields (`type PlanId = string & { readonly _: unique symbol }`).

Worth noting while you're here: nothing escapes the separator, so `plan: "pro:eu"` yields an ambiguous key. Out of scope for the finding, but it's the same "the format is not a contract" root cause.

## 3. `canRunBilling` — Negative Flag Maze (+ Unprefixed Boolean)

**Evidence:** `!disableBilling && !skipRetry`; both names unprefixed at L34.

Findings 3 and 4 are one defect. Reading the current line out loud: *"billing can run when billing is not disabled and retry is not skipped."* Two negations and a positive question.

The behaviour-preserving fix — same truth table, readable, and it kills both findings:

```ts
export interface BillingPolicy {
  isBillingEnabled: boolean;
  isRetryEnabled: boolean;
}

export function canRunBilling({ isBillingEnabled, isRetryEnabled }: BillingPolicy): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryEnabled;
}
```

(`if (x) return true; return false;` is just `return x`.)

**Flag separately, don't fold into the refactor:** `skipRetry` is not a billing-eligibility concern. As written, a caller who merely wants to disable retries gets billing switched off entirely. Once the negations are gone that reads as an obvious bug rather than a plausible-looking conjunction — which is exactly what the maze was hiding. If it's unintentional, the follow-up is to split the predicates:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(p: BillingPolicy) { return p.isBillingEnabled }
export function canRetryBilling(p: BillingPolicy) { return p.isBillingEnabled && p.isRetryEnabled }
```

**What an agent gets wrong today.** Asked to *"also block billing when the account is suspended,"* it extends the chain to `!disableBilling && !skipRetry && !suspended` — three negations, and the odds of getting De Morgan right on the next edit drop again. Worse, the object parameter matters as much as the renaming: today's two positional booleans mean `canRunBilling(true, false)` is a call-site swap away from inverting the meaning, and it compiles. Inverting the flags flips the required literal at every call site — free here (no callers), but these are exported, so pin the change in a release note if anything external consumes them.

---

**Sequencing.** All three change public signatures against a file with no tests. Write the characterization tests first — specifically `describeCheckout`'s exact output string, and `resolvePaymentResult`'s error-before-redirect precedence — then refactor. Order them by `agent_risk`: `resolvePaymentResult` first (it's the one where an extension silently produces a fourth uncheckable shape), then `describeCheckout`, then the flags. I haven't touched the fixture; editing it would move the eval baseline.
